1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,267
Here is a strange fact about the history
of cutting tools.

2
00:00:05,617 --> 00:00:11,675
The man who started the modern tool steel
revolution never patented his invention.

3
00:00:12,025 --> 00:00:13,540
He kept it secret.

4
00:00:13,890 --> 00:00:18,199
He bought his ingredients through
middlemen and gave them code names.

5
00:00:18,549 --> 00:00:23,797
And the steel he made still shaped how
metal is cut for generations after him.

6
00:00:24,947 --> 00:00:27,849
His name was Robert Forester Mushet.

7
00:00:28,199 --> 00:00:36,115
In 1868, in the Forest of Dean, he made a
steel that did something no steel had done

8
00:00:36,115 --> 00:00:36,775
before.

9
00:00:37,125 --> 00:00:39,023
It hardened by itself.

10
00:00:39,373 --> 00:00:42,893
Every blacksmith and toolmaker knew the
old way.

11
00:00:43,243 --> 00:00:47,958
You heated the tool red hot, then plunged
it into water or oil.

12
00:00:49,108 --> 00:00:52,009
The sudden chill locked in the hardness.

13
00:00:52,359 --> 00:00:56,967
It was an abrupt and risky step, and it
was the heart of the craft.

14
00:00:57,317 --> 00:00:59,216
Mushet's steel skipped it.

15
00:00:59,566 --> 00:01:04,302
You forged the tool, you let it cool in
plain air, and it came out hard.

16
00:01:04,652 --> 00:01:07,276
It was the first steel of its kind.

17
00:01:08,426 --> 00:01:13,610
Later it was overtaken by a steel built on
the very same idea.

18
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:18,141
Today few people outside the metal trades
know his name.

19
00:01:18,491 --> 00:01:21,030
So what happened to Mushet steel?

20
00:01:22,980 --> 00:01:28,104
To see why this mattered, we need to look
at what happens at the tip of a cutting

21
00:01:28,104 --> 00:01:28,420
tool.

22
00:01:28,770 --> 00:01:33,549
A lathe tool does not slice metal the way
a knife slices bread.

23
00:01:33,899 --> 00:01:39,403
It pushes into the work and forces a layer
of metal to shear away as a chip.

24
00:01:39,753 --> 00:01:43,827
That chip slides over the face of the tool
under great pressure.

25
00:01:44,177 --> 00:01:49,127
The friction and the bending of the metal
turn much of that effort into heat.

26
00:01:50,277 --> 00:01:54,373
And much of that heat collects in a small
patch right at the edge.

27
00:01:56,323 --> 00:02:01,293
For most of the nineteenth century, tools
were made of plain carbon steel.

28
00:02:01,643 --> 00:02:06,038
Carbon steel can be made very hard by
heating and quenching.

29
00:02:06,388 --> 00:02:08,863
But that hardness has a weakness.

30
00:02:09,213 --> 00:02:12,007
Heat the tool again, and it can soften.

31
00:02:12,357 --> 00:02:17,221
So a cutting edge in a carbon steel tool
is always fighting itself.

32
00:02:18,371 --> 00:02:23,619
The harder you push the cut, the hotter
the edge gets, and the faster it softens.

33
00:02:23,969 --> 00:02:26,615
The machinist's answer was patience.

34
00:02:26,965 --> 00:02:28,351
Take light cuts.

35
00:02:28,701 --> 00:02:30,045
Run slowly.

36
00:02:30,395 --> 00:02:31,697
Stop often.

37
00:02:32,847 --> 00:02:37,476
Keep the tool cool and sharp, and never
let the edge change color.

38
00:02:37,826 --> 00:02:40,642
That habit set the pace of the shop floor.

39
00:02:40,992 --> 00:02:45,895
It limited how fast a shaft could be
turned, how fast a cylinder could be

40
00:02:45,895 --> 00:02:49,589
bored, and how many parts a shop could
finish in a day.

41
00:02:49,939 --> 00:02:53,353
A steel that could cut faster would be
valuable.

42
00:02:53,703 --> 00:02:55,196
Mushet found one.

43
00:02:56,346 --> 00:02:58,991
He just never collected the fortune.

44
00:03:00,941 --> 00:03:08,064
Robert Forester Mushet was born on the
eighth of April, 1811, in Coleford, in the

45
00:03:08,064 --> 00:03:11,053
Forest of Dean in Gloucestershire.

46
00:03:11,403 --> 00:03:14,262
He was the youngest son of Scottish
parents.

47
00:03:14,612 --> 00:03:19,898
His father, David Mushet, was an
ironmaster, and the family business was

48
00:03:19,898 --> 00:03:20,265
iron.

49
00:03:20,615 --> 00:03:28,807
David built the Darkhill Ironworks around
1818 or 1819.

50
00:03:29,157 --> 00:03:34,427
Robert took over its management in 1845.

51
00:03:35,577 --> 00:03:40,995
In 1848 he moved to the Forest Steel
Works.

52
00:03:41,345 --> 00:03:46,657
There, by one account, he ran more than
ten thousand experiments in ten years.

53
00:03:47,007 --> 00:03:48,949
Think about what that means.

54
00:03:49,299 --> 00:03:53,331
He was not a university professor with a
laboratory budget.

55
00:03:53,681 --> 00:03:59,045
He was a working ironmaster in a rural
valley, melting small batches, testing

56
00:03:59,045 --> 00:04:02,598
them, writing down the results, and doing
it again.

57
00:04:03,748 --> 00:04:06,564
Ten thousand tests, by that account.

58
00:04:06,914 --> 00:04:11,799
It was the kind of patient, practical work
that built the nineteenth century.

59
00:04:12,149 --> 00:04:17,333
And it led him first to a problem that had
nothing to do with cutting tools at all.

60
00:04:17,683 --> 00:04:21,545
It was the problem that was holding back
the age of steel.

61
00:04:23,495 --> 00:04:30,819
In 1856, Henry Bessemer described his new
steelmaking process to the British

62
00:04:30,819 --> 00:04:34,673
Association, at a meeting in Cheltenham.

63
00:04:35,023 --> 00:04:37,050
The idea was bold.

64
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:42,904
Blow air through molten pig iron, and the
oxygen burns out the silicon, the

65
00:04:42,904 --> 00:04:44,739
manganese and the carbon.

66
00:04:45,089 --> 00:04:49,441
Bessemer was after cheap steel, and this
was a way to get it.

67
00:04:49,791 --> 00:04:51,241
But there was a catch.

68
00:04:52,391 --> 00:04:55,250
The metal that came out was often poor.

69
00:04:55,600 --> 00:04:57,200
Mushet had a fix.

70
00:04:57,550 --> 00:05:02,414
He showed that you could blow the iron
until nearly all the carbon was gone.

71
00:05:02,764 --> 00:05:07,692
Then you added back a measured amount of a
material called spiegeleisen.

72
00:05:08,842 --> 00:05:12,170
The name is German for looking-glass iron.

73
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:16,744
It is an alloy of iron and manganese, with
a little carbon.

74
00:05:17,094 --> 00:05:22,501
The addition restored the carbon in a
controlled way, and the manganese made the

75
00:05:22,501 --> 00:05:23,515
steel workable.

76
00:05:23,865 --> 00:05:28,580
Mushet's patent on the method lapsed,
because he could not pay the fees.

77
00:05:29,730 --> 00:05:31,629
Bessemer took it over.

78
00:05:31,979 --> 00:05:38,037
By 1866, Mushet was destitute and in poor
health.

79
00:05:38,387 --> 00:05:43,606
That year, his sixteen-year-old daughter
Mary travelled to London alone and

80
00:05:43,606 --> 00:05:48,755
confronted Bessemer, arguing that his
success rested on her father's work.

81
00:05:49,105 --> 00:05:54,417
Bessemer then agreed to pay Mushet a
pension of three hundred pounds a year.

82
00:05:54,767 --> 00:05:57,221
That was a very considerable sum.

83
00:05:58,371 --> 00:06:03,043
He paid it for more than twenty years,
possibly to head off legal action.

84
00:06:04,993 --> 00:06:12,118
In 1857, Mushet also made some of the
first durable steel rails.

85
00:06:12,468 --> 00:06:19,310
One rail at Derby Midland station was
still in good condition in 1863, even

86
00:06:19,310 --> 00:06:24,692
though about seven hundred trains passed
over it every day.

87
00:06:25,042 --> 00:06:32,116
In 1868, Mushet came back to the
experiment that mattered most for our

88
00:06:32,116 --> 00:06:32,722
story.

89
00:06:33,072 --> 00:06:38,149
A manufacturer in Glasgow had asked him to
produce a new cast steel.

90
00:06:38,499 --> 00:06:42,169
The steel he made was too brittle for
cutting tools.

91
00:06:43,319 --> 00:06:46,711
So Mushet kept experimenting with other
alloys.

92
00:06:47,061 --> 00:06:49,642
One of them was a tungsten steel.

93
00:06:49,992 --> 00:06:54,664
And, unexpectedly, it hardened when it
cooled in air after forging.

94
00:06:55,014 --> 00:07:00,603
Tungsten was the main alloying element,
somewhere between four and twelve percent.

95
00:07:01,753 --> 00:07:06,955
Manganese ran from about two to four
percent, and carbon from about one and a

96
00:07:06,955 --> 00:07:09,049
half to two and a half percent.

97
00:07:09,399 --> 00:07:15,244
A typical sample held about nine percent
tungsten, two and a half percent manganese

98
00:07:15,244 --> 00:07:18,061
and one and eight tenths percent carbon.

99
00:07:18,411 --> 00:07:22,016
Compare that with the carbon steel in a
normal shop.

100
00:07:22,366 --> 00:07:27,422
It was mostly iron and carbon, with only
small amounts of anything else.

101
00:07:27,772 --> 00:07:30,972
Mushet's steel was a different kind of
material.

102
00:07:32,122 --> 00:07:34,362
It was a true alloy steel.

103
00:07:34,712 --> 00:07:37,421
And he told no one how to make it.

104
00:07:37,771 --> 00:07:39,371
He did not patent it.

105
00:07:39,721 --> 00:07:44,393
Ingredients were bought through
intermediaries and called by code words.

106
00:07:44,743 --> 00:07:48,690
Mixing was done at his steelworks in the
Forest of Dean.

107
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:55,049
The mixed batches were shipped in barrels
to Samuel Osborn and Company in Sheffield,

108
00:07:55,049 --> 00:07:56,475
where they were melted.

109
00:07:56,825 --> 00:08:00,238
It was sold as Robert Mushet's Special
Steel.

110
00:08:00,588 --> 00:08:03,191
Some called it self-hardening steel.

111
00:08:03,541 --> 00:08:06,143
Others called it air-hardening steel.

112
00:08:08,093 --> 00:08:13,362
Now let us look at the engineering,
because this is where the idea is easy to

113
00:08:13,362 --> 00:08:13,704
miss.

114
00:08:14,054 --> 00:08:18,470
In a plain carbon steel, hardness is
something you have to trap.

115
00:08:18,820 --> 00:08:22,127
You heat the steel until its structure
changes.

116
00:08:22,477 --> 00:08:27,551
Then you cool it so fast that the
structure has no time to settle into its

117
00:08:27,551 --> 00:08:28,237
soft form.

118
00:08:28,587 --> 00:08:30,144
Water does that.

119
00:08:31,294 --> 00:08:32,638
Oil does that.

120
00:08:32,988 --> 00:08:34,353
Air does not.

121
00:08:34,703 --> 00:08:39,887
Air is far too slow, so carbon steel left
to cool in air stays soft.

122
00:08:40,237 --> 00:08:42,925
Mushet's steel behaved differently.

123
00:08:43,275 --> 00:08:48,831
Its alloying elements, mainly tungsten and
manganese, changed how it responded to

124
00:08:48,831 --> 00:08:52,534
cooling, so that ordinary air was enough
to harden it.

125
00:08:53,684 --> 00:08:56,863
The tool hardened as it cooled off the
forge.

126
00:08:57,213 --> 00:08:59,431
That mattered in two ways.

127
00:08:59,781 --> 00:09:02,427
First, it made the tool easier to make.

128
00:09:02,777 --> 00:09:07,598
A toolmaker could forge the shape and let
it cool, without a water quench.

129
00:09:07,948 --> 00:09:10,465
Mushet's steel removed that step.

130
00:09:11,615 --> 00:09:15,946
Second, and more important, it made the
tool better at its work.

131
00:09:16,296 --> 00:09:21,203
One modern account says it was harder than
ordinary water-quenched steel.

132
00:09:21,553 --> 00:09:23,793
It was also non-magnetic.

133
00:09:24,143 --> 00:09:27,087
The steel came to the shop already hard.

134
00:09:27,437 --> 00:09:30,978
It was made for one job, and that job was
cutting.

135
00:09:32,928 --> 00:09:36,192
We know how good it was because someone
measured it.

136
00:09:36,542 --> 00:09:44,577
In 1894, the American engineer Frederick
Winslow Taylor tested Mushet steel against

137
00:09:44,577 --> 00:09:46,803
high-carbon tool steel.

138
00:09:47,153 --> 00:09:52,849
On hard tire-steel forgings, it cut
forty-one to forty-seven percent faster.

139
00:09:53,199 --> 00:09:57,103
On mild steels, it cut about ninety
percent faster.

140
00:09:57,453 --> 00:10:02,772
And when a stream of water was used as a
cutting fluid, the speed could go up by

141
00:10:02,772 --> 00:10:04,301
another thirty percent.

142
00:10:05,451 --> 00:10:08,289
Think about a change of ninety percent.

143
00:10:08,639 --> 00:10:12,095
A job that took ten hours now took about
five.

144
00:10:12,445 --> 00:10:14,962
No new machine had been built.

145
00:10:15,312 --> 00:10:17,936
No new motor had been installed.

146
00:10:18,286 --> 00:10:21,038
Only the steel at the tip had changed.

147
00:10:22,188 --> 00:10:26,348
Taylor became a famous name in the story
of cutting speed.

148
00:10:26,698 --> 00:10:28,810
But he was not starting from nothing.

149
00:10:29,160 --> 00:10:30,995
He started from Mushet.

150
00:10:32,945 --> 00:10:38,611
Mushet steel needed someone to make it at
scale, and that someone was a Sheffield

151
00:10:38,611 --> 00:10:38,961
firm.

152
00:10:39,311 --> 00:10:46,922
Samuel Osborn had set up his company in
1851 at the Clyde Works in Sheffield,

153
00:10:46,922 --> 00:10:48,207
making files.

154
00:10:48,557 --> 00:10:55,378
In 1870, according to the Science Museum
Group, Osborn became the exclusive

155
00:10:55,378 --> 00:10:58,562
producer of Mushet's special steel.

156
00:10:58,912 --> 00:11:00,277
The firm grew.

157
00:11:00,627 --> 00:11:06,133
At its peak, according to the same source,
it virtually monopolised the world's

158
00:11:06,133 --> 00:11:07,945
manufacture of tool steel.

159
00:11:09,095 --> 00:11:14,343
That is a big claim, and it comes from a
single source, so treat it with care.

160
00:11:14,693 --> 00:11:16,890
But the direction is clear.

161
00:11:17,240 --> 00:11:19,971
Osborn tied its name to Mushet's steel.

162
00:11:21,121 --> 00:11:29,505
In 1876, the Iron and Steel Institute
awarded Mushet the Bessemer Gold Medal.

163
00:11:29,855 --> 00:11:34,783
It was a late recognition for a man who
had once been left destitute.

164
00:11:35,133 --> 00:11:41,533
Another of his ideas, from 1861, is still
around today.

165
00:11:41,883 --> 00:11:43,675
It is called the dozzle.

166
00:11:44,025 --> 00:11:49,648
It is a clay cone set in the top of an
ingot mould, and it is the forerunner of

167
00:11:49,648 --> 00:11:50,787
today's hot top.

168
00:11:51,937 --> 00:11:55,820
Today the idea is called a hot top, or a
feeder head.

169
00:11:56,170 --> 00:12:03,317
Mushet died in Cheltenham on the
twenty-ninth of January, 1891.

170
00:12:03,667 --> 00:12:05,779
He was seventy-nine years old.

171
00:12:07,729 --> 00:12:10,801
Mushet did not live to see what came next.

172
00:12:11,151 --> 00:12:15,140
And what came next was built directly on
Mushet steel.

173
00:12:15,490 --> 00:12:22,757
In 1898, Frederick Winslow Taylor joined
the Bethlehem Steel Company in

174
00:12:22,757 --> 00:12:24,087
Pennsylvania.

175
00:12:24,437 --> 00:12:32,972
Between 1898 and 1900, he and a colleague,
Maunsel White, ran a huge program of tests

176
00:12:32,972 --> 00:12:35,381
on tungsten tool steels.

177
00:12:36,531 --> 00:12:40,606
They worked on Mushet steel and other
self-hardening steels.

178
00:12:40,956 --> 00:12:46,047
They found that heating the steel to a
much higher temperature than the industry

179
00:12:46,047 --> 00:12:48,465
thought desirable gave better results.

180
00:12:48,815 --> 00:12:54,170
One history of the steel says the key
change was adding about three point eight

181
00:12:54,170 --> 00:12:58,373
percent chromium to a steel with about
eight percent tungsten.

182
00:12:58,723 --> 00:13:00,749
The effect was dramatic.

183
00:13:01,899 --> 00:13:07,403
Cutting speed rose from about thirty
surface feet per minute to about ninety.

184
00:13:07,753 --> 00:13:09,951
That is three times faster.

185
00:13:10,301 --> 00:13:16,123
At the Paris Exposition of 1900, a
demonstration of cutting tools made from

186
00:13:16,123 --> 00:13:18,685
the new steel caused a sensation.

187
00:13:19,035 --> 00:13:22,640
This was the start of what we call
high-speed steel.

188
00:13:22,990 --> 00:13:24,803
Notice what happened.

189
00:13:25,953 --> 00:13:29,921
Mushet had made the first steel that
hardened without a quench.

190
00:13:30,271 --> 00:13:35,612
Taylor and White took that kind of steel,
changed the alloy, and heat treated it at

191
00:13:35,612 --> 00:13:38,250
a much higher temperature than was usual.

192
00:13:38,600 --> 00:13:42,760
The same idea, pushed further, cut three
times as fast.

193
00:13:43,110 --> 00:13:46,182
For Taylor and White, it was also a
business.

194
00:13:46,532 --> 00:13:51,993
The two men received one hundred thousand
dollars for the English patents alone.

195
00:13:53,143 --> 00:13:56,578
The American patent was eventually
nullified.

196
00:13:58,528 --> 00:14:02,240
So why did Mushet steel disappear from the
lathe?

197
00:14:02,590 --> 00:14:08,265
The honest answer is that the sources give
us the shape of it, but not every detail.

198
00:14:08,615 --> 00:14:10,641
Here is what we can say.

199
00:14:10,991 --> 00:14:13,274
First, the better steel arrived.

200
00:14:13,624 --> 00:14:19,433
Once Taylor and White's method was known,
a tool that cut about three times as fast

201
00:14:19,433 --> 00:14:21,603
made the older steel look slow.

202
00:14:22,753 --> 00:14:25,291
Second, the idea kept improving.

203
00:14:25,641 --> 00:14:33,549
A steel called T1, a tungsten grade, was
formally classified as high-speed steel in

204
00:14:33,549 --> 00:14:34,025
1910.

205
00:14:34,375 --> 00:14:38,941
It was patented by the Crucible Steel
Company of New Jersey.

206
00:14:39,291 --> 00:14:42,960
Later, M2 became the most widely used
grade.

207
00:14:43,310 --> 00:14:49,219
Cobalt was added to it to give steels like
M35 and M42.

208
00:14:50,369 --> 00:14:53,932
Third, Mushet had chosen not to patent his
steel.

209
00:14:54,282 --> 00:14:57,909
He relied on secrecy, and it did not make
him rich.

210
00:14:58,259 --> 00:15:03,460
The composition of his steel has since
been analyzed, although the exact

211
00:15:03,460 --> 00:15:06,856
production process is still said to be
unknown.

212
00:15:07,206 --> 00:15:09,979
None of this is a story of failure.

213
00:15:10,329 --> 00:15:15,556
It is a story of a good idea being passed
on until it became ordinary.

214
00:15:17,506 --> 00:15:21,282
Mushet steel itself is long gone from the
shop floor.

215
00:15:21,632 --> 00:15:24,064
But its descendants are everywhere.

216
00:15:24,414 --> 00:15:29,619
High-speed steel is still one of the most
common tool materials in the world.

217
00:15:29,969 --> 00:15:34,215
The grade M2 is used for drill bits, taps
and reamers.

218
00:15:34,565 --> 00:15:39,821
Many of the drills in a modern toolbox are
made from a steel that traces back to

219
00:15:39,821 --> 00:15:42,778
Taylor and White, and through them to
Mushet.

220
00:15:43,928 --> 00:15:46,467
The name itself lasted too.

221
00:15:46,817 --> 00:15:52,747
Samuel Osborn and Company of Sheffield
sold a range of high-speed steels under

222
00:15:52,747 --> 00:15:53,963
the Mushet name.

223
00:15:54,313 --> 00:15:59,497
There was Double Mushet ND, which matches
the American grade T1.

224
00:15:59,847 --> 00:16:03,943
There was Triple Mushet GZ, which matches
T4.

225
00:16:04,293 --> 00:16:08,176
There was Mushet MKK, which matches M2.

226
00:16:09,326 --> 00:16:13,763
And there was Mushet Special VG, which
matches M15.

227
00:16:14,113 --> 00:16:19,410
So even after the original secret steel
was gone, a customer could still buy a

228
00:16:19,410 --> 00:16:22,263
high-speed steel with Mushet's name on it.

229
00:16:22,613 --> 00:16:24,362
It was a different steel.

230
00:16:24,712 --> 00:16:26,461
But it carried his name.

231
00:16:26,811 --> 00:16:29,819
His other work is still in use as well.

232
00:16:30,969 --> 00:16:36,303
The hot top on a steel ingot mould is a
direct descendant of the dozzle.

233
00:16:36,653 --> 00:16:42,095
And his spiegeleisen step improved the
quality of Bessemer steel in the first

234
00:16:42,095 --> 00:16:42,519
place.

235
00:16:44,469 --> 00:16:50,140
Mushet steel teaches a few things that
every machinist and every engineer should

236
00:16:50,140 --> 00:16:51,061
keep in mind.

237
00:16:51,411 --> 00:16:55,465
The first is that the great step is often
the first one.

238
00:16:55,815 --> 00:16:58,609
Mushet did not make the fastest steel.

239
00:16:58,959 --> 00:17:04,131
He made the first steel that hardened by
itself, and everything that followed grew

240
00:17:04,131 --> 00:17:04,762
from that.

241
00:17:05,112 --> 00:17:07,715
Taylor and White improved on it.

242
00:17:08,865 --> 00:17:10,785
They did not invent it.

243
00:17:11,135 --> 00:17:15,935
The second is that a secret is a poor way
to protect an idea.

244
00:17:16,285 --> 00:17:20,573
Mushet kept the recipe locked away, and it
did not make him rich.

245
00:17:20,923 --> 00:17:25,690
Taylor and White were paid one hundred
thousand dollars for their English

246
00:17:25,690 --> 00:17:28,368
patents, and still lost the American one.

247
00:17:28,718 --> 00:17:32,046
There is no easy lesson about patents
here.

248
00:17:33,196 --> 00:17:38,030
But it is a reminder that the person who
makes the breakthrough is rarely the

249
00:17:38,030 --> 00:17:39,788
person who gets paid for it.

250
00:17:40,138 --> 00:17:42,293
The third is the simplest.

251
00:17:42,643 --> 00:17:46,376
A cutting edge is a hot, small,
unforgiving place.

252
00:17:46,726 --> 00:17:51,505
Every material that works there has to
stay hard when the heat arrives.

253
00:17:51,855 --> 00:17:58,184
Robert Mushet reportedly ran more than ten
thousand experiments in a Forest of Dean

254
00:17:58,184 --> 00:17:59,023
steelworks.

255
00:18:00,173 --> 00:18:07,796
He solved a key quality problem in the
Bessemer process, and by 1866 he was

256
00:18:07,796 --> 00:18:08,813
destitute.

257
00:18:09,163 --> 00:18:12,875
Then he made the first tool steel that
hardened in air.

258
00:18:13,225 --> 00:18:16,937
The steel in a modern drill bit owes him a
debt.

259
00:18:17,287 --> 00:18:20,017
That is what happened to Mushet steel.

260
00:18:20,367 --> 00:18:26,094
If you want to see what came after it, how
the Taylor and White heat treatment really

261
00:18:26,094 --> 00:18:31,348
worked, and why Paris in 1900 stunned the
engineers who saw it, stay with this

262
00:18:31,348 --> 00:18:31,887
channel.

263
00:18:33,037 --> 00:18:37,923
And tell us in the comments which
forgotten tool we should open up next.

